        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Israel's double game between negotiations with Lebanon and occupation in the south Middle East Issued on: 19/04/2026 - 14:33 A ceasefire agreement between Israel and Lebanon was reached following a first round of talks in Washington. Hussein Ibish, Senior Resident Scholar at the Arab Gulf States Institute in Washington, analyses Israel’s strategy in Lebanon, which he describes as oscillating between negotiations and continued presence in the south. He notes that Israel repeatedly states its intention to push out Hezbollah, as a justification for expanding control and establishing a buffer zone up to the Litani River. He also analyses the latest developments between Iran and the US. Food security under threat as Middle East war disrupts fertiliser supply 19/04/2026 Middle East A petition called 'Lebanon will not be the next Gaza' calls on French leaders to take action 19/04/2026 Middle East Iran: Prolonged conflict reshapes view of the regime, analyst says 19/04/2026 Middle East Middle East: Hezbollah blamed after French peacekeeper killed in Lebanon 19/04/2026 Middle East What is UNIFIL? the UN mission in southern Lebanon explained 19/04/2026 Middle East Strait of Hormuz: Open, closed, open, closed - What's actually happening? 18/04/2026 Middle East Replay : Donald Trump holds briefing amid Hormuz tension 18/04/2026 Middle East UNIFIL sheds light on southern Lebanon attack that killed French soldier 18/04/2026 Middle East No signs of regime collapse as Iran weathers weeks of war 18/04/2026 Middle East"
